@prefix sensing: <http://example.org/agora/sensing#> .
@prefix mqtt: <http://example.org/agora/mqtt#> .
@prefix ag:    <http://example.org/agora#> .
@prefix rdfs:  <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix owl:   <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix codec: <http://example.org/agora/codec#> .
@prefix : <http://example.org/agora/codec#> .

<http://example.org/agora/codec> a owl:Ontology ;
    rdfs:label "Agora codecs" ;
    rdfs:comment """How bytes become a DOCUMENT — the first of the three stages a reading passes through, the others being the pointer that says which value in that document is a given sensor's and the calibration that turns a raw number into a quantity. A family with interchangeable members, borne by a BINDING rather than by an agent: which format a device speaks is something only the device can say, so it is stated on the device and the member serving it is DERIVED at genesis onto the CHANNEL that device sends on, rather than onto anyone's capabilities. The stream and not the sensor, because three sensors sharing one topic would otherwise carry three copies of one fact with nothing comparing them, and because a command channel has no sensor at all. Same mechanism as a capability and the same premise-then-conclusion discipline; what differs is the bearer and the predicate, not when it is decided. See knowledge/decisions/bytes-become-a-quantity-in-stages.md.""" .

#################  The family  #################

:Encoding a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Encoding" ;
    rdfs:comment "A wire format. Anything that turns bytes into a document is one of these, so a binding may name what it speaks without knowing which package answers. Note what is NOT here: which value in the document is anyone's. That is mqtt:readingPointer, and it is deliberately not a member of any family — RFC 6901 works over any tree, so a pointer written today keeps meaning the same thing whichever member below produced the document. That indifference is what makes declaring these two cost nothing to defer." .

:encoding a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:range :Encoding ;
    rdfs:label "encoding" ;
    rdfs:comment "THE PREMISE, stated by the world: what format this device's payloads are in. A fact about the board's firmware, like sensing:senseMode, and the only half of this a world may write. ABSENT means codec:Json, which is what every board here already sends — so no shipped world states one." .

:decodedBy a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:range :Encoding ;
    rdfs:label "decoded by" ;
    rdfs:comment "THE CONCLUSION, derived at genesis by rules.ru and never written by hand: which codec actually serves this CHANNEL. Two predicates rather than one because a world states what is TRUE of a device and genesis writes down what SERVES it — collapsing them would let a world state its own conclusion. Deriving it also puts the DEFAULT in the graph: before this, every sensor in every world was decoded as JSON by a flag on a Python class, which was true, load-bearing and unreadable by anything. A sensor's pipeline is now something you can query rather than something you infer from which classes a build imported — read through mqtt:publishesOn, since the fact belongs to the stream." .

#################  The members  #################

:Json a :Encoding ; rdfs:label "JSON" ;
    rdfs:comment "The one member with an implementation, and the default. Every board in every shipped world publishes a JSON object and is instructed with one. It is a CODEC rather than a parser because it codes both directions — though only DECODING is on a live path today: outbound messages are still serialised by agent/runtime.py, which json.dumps every publication whether it is a cadence, an offer or a claim. So encode() is implemented, tested and unreached, and a society whose boards spoke CBOR would have to route that path through here too. See the seams in knowledge/decisions/bytes-become-a-quantity-in-stages.md." .

:Cbor a :Encoding ; rdfs:label "CBOR" ;
    rdfs:comment "Declared, unimplemented. RFC 8949 — the same data model as JSON in a compact binary form, which is what a battery-powered board would reach for to shorten its radio time. Named first among the unbuilt because it is the cheapest: CBOR decodes to exactly the maps and arrays a pointer already walks, so a member would be a decode call and nothing else." .

:Kaitai a :Encoding ; rdfs:label "Kaitai Struct" ;
    rdfs:comment "Declared, unimplemented. For a device that speaks a genuine BINARY protocol rather than a serialisation format — a packed struct of fixed-width fields, described declaratively in a .ksy spec rather than parsed by hand. Worth naming because it is the case that looks like it would break the design and does not: what a Kaitai spec yields is a TREE, so mqtt:readingPointer addresses into it unchanged. A codec is where a binary format would land, and no other stage would move." .
